        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4cc18ca9-aa88-5467-a2f0-988e07ba0508",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Works: Louise, Le roi malgré lui, Julien, Amica\n- People: Gustave Charpentier, Saint-Pol-Roux, Emmanuel Chabrier, Emile de Najac, Paul Burani, Charles Rousselière, Pietro Mascagni\n- Concepts: opera, opéra-comique, sequel, world premiere\n\nFacts:\n- In 1887, Emmanuel Chabrier's Le roi malgré lui was presented as an opéra-comique in three acts with an original libretto by Emile de Najac and Paul Burani.\n- In 1900, Gustave Charpentier's Louise was an opera in four acts with an original French libretto by Charpentier, with some contributions by the symbolist poet Saint-Pol-Roux.\n- Gustave Charpentier's Julien is a sequel to Louise and describes the artistic aspirations of Louise's suitor Julien.\n- Charles Rousselière sang in the world premieres of Gustave Charpentier's Julien in the title role and Pietro Mascagni's Amica as Giorgio.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b7efc224-7b10-526b-a15f-489bc3a65d6a",
        "text": "Page: Louise (opera). Sentence: Louise is an opera (\"roman musical\" ) in four acts by Gustave Charpentier to an original French libretto by the composer, with some contributions by Saint-Pol-Roux, a symbolist poet and inspiration of the surrealists.\nPage: Le roi malgré lui. Sentence: Le roi malgré lui (\"King in Spite of Himself\" or \"The reluctant king\") is an opéra-comique in three acts by Emmanuel Chabrier of 1887 with an original libretto by Emile de Najac and Paul Burani.\nPage: Julien (opera). Sentence: \"Julien\" is a sequel to Charpentier's \"Louise\" (1900) and describes the artistic aspirations of Louise’s suitor Julien.\nPage: Charles Rousselière. Sentence: He sang in the world premieres of several operas, including the title role in Charpentier's \"Julien\" and Giorgio in Mascagni's \"Amica\"",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
